ally there  in the woods and they are used to  control access to the woods a lot of the  time so not all woods have graves in  them but most graves at least until  about six  700 have woods around them and so access  to the grave sites is not just about  ritual it's also about access to  firewood do they still have the grave  graveyards in woods today so this is  still a strong tradition but it's become  um maybe superficial is too strong a  word so part of it and this is one of  the questions that I don't know that I'm  totally able to answer is win these  woods get cut down I tend to think it's  largely in the nineteenth and twentieth  century when China goes through a whole  series of crises but as late as 1800  Western travelers are still describing  this is what Chinese graves look like  especially in the South there they've  got woods behind them but then you know  in the modern period some of this  preference to bury near trees becomes  sort of formal formulaic and has  actually very little to do with the wood  so you'll see people putting like  plastic trees next to their graves or  you'll even see people making online  memorials to their ancestors and there  will be like tree gifts or whatever next  to it so the tradition persists but it's  changed a bit and part of that is that  in the modern world access the firewood  doesn't matter the same way it did prior  to say the late 19th century all right  China is a huge country so I am curious  you know if you are you looking at all  of China or a particular part of China  and if you know and if it's all over our  things similar just similar in some ways  yeah so I've done fieldwork principally  in two provinces who again and Jung see  which are in the south fujian is on the  coast you see is is the next sort of  most interior province and really the  story that I can tell mostly focuses on  the south and on in particular on wood  exporting regions of the south north  china is very different so the North  China Plain is has very little wood okay  from probably quite early in history and  then when we're talking about Manchuria  the Northeast that historically was home  to non Semitic people's to Manchus and  Mongols and so with the last imperial  dynasty in china this was actually a  Manchu controlled dynasty and so they  had very different forms of Land  Management for the Manchu lands in the  Northeast then for the Chinese lands in  the rest of the country so yeah my study  primarily focuses on the south okay all  right so you talk about warring states  philosophers and when you discuss forest  regulation so what effect did these  philosophers have on later policies sure  so this is one of the most interesting  things about looking at policy in China  so the warring States is a period from  sort of the fourth or fifth century to  the 3rd century BC so this is almost two  millennia before the period that's the  main focus of my research but my the  poly the policy makers in the 15th 16th  17th century are still using the  arguments of the warring States of the  third fourth fifth century BC to make  their arguments and you is there a  reason why that is do you think I mean  do you have a kind of timeless as to why  that would be so I think that there are  two reasons so the first is that  political philosophy in China basically  starts in the warring States and a lot  of that is because this is the period  when States the period the early period  of State Building in China and so with  the emergence of recognizable forms of  polity you also have the recognizable  emergence of forms of political  discourse and so up until the 19th even  early 20th century these ideas still  have some currency because the  institution's themselves actually have a  great deal of continuity going back to  millennia the other reason which is very  actually closely intertwined to the rise  of states is this is the earliest period  where there's a clear experience of  environmental degradation of  deforestation in particular and so not  only are these early philosophers  reacting to the rise of states but  they're also reacting to the possibility  of environmental degradation and so the  early States um these warring States and  then the earliest empires in China are  able to draw on this body of philosophy  to argue for certain forms of  conservation right okay and so you know  it's not just the earliest policy  arguments in general that go back to the  warring States but it's specifically the  earliest environmental policy arguments  that go back to the warring States okay  and so those are the these same ideas  that are getting I mean I guess in some  ways it's the Silent Spring moment right  it's the the moment when they realize  the possibility of totally degrading  their landscape right and so the  political will and the ideas about how  to use this political will to create  sustainability are created right right  you know so huh and listening to you say  that happening you know so many  thousands of years ago it makes me think  about today in terms of you know some of  the things we're facing in our world  today in terms of deforestation and  environmental issues so I guess I want  to ask you does the early history of  Forestry in China teaches anything for  today yeah um I think it does I mean we  have to be careful applying the lessons  of history that there's a phrase I turn  a phrase I really like which is history  doesn't repeat itself but it rhymes  right and so some of these same issues  that we see at least in general terms in  as early as you know the 3rd or 4th  century BC are the same issues again in  general terms that we see today and so  the the interplay between questions of  preventing environmental degradation and  questions of ensuring equitable access  to resources are things that people were  dealing with for  hundreds and really thousands of years  in China and that we're dealing with now  and so when we talk about deforestation  in the contemporary world the places  that were most concerned about  deforestation its not New England it's  not Germany right it's it's not really  even China anymore it's largely places  like Indonesia Brazil right and these  are by and large poor countries and the  regions that are being deforested are by  and large poor regions and so when we're  talking about halting this deforestation  and encouraging sustainability in the  Brazilian rain forest we also have to  think about what effect this has on the  locals who rely on forest resources for  their subsistence right it is a  complicated situation you know in  addition to the people of the animals  that are displaced as well so it is it  is a very complicated situation and so I  think one of the things that that doing  this study has has taught me is that  sustainability is absolutely an  important goal right but we also have to  be aware of what people are doing in the  name of sustainability we need to be  aware of when governments are using  sustainability as an excuse to claim  resources for themselves or even when  corporations are using sustainability as  a resource as an excuse to claim  resources for themselves saying you know  these these peasants don't know what  they're doing they are burning the  forest down let us take care of it we  know how to manage the resources  efficiently and so this is something  that I mean I see myself continuing to  be concerned with even as I move past  this specific project is this question  of the interplay between sustainability  and equity in access to resources right  so more to come I hope so yes thank you  so much for being here with us today  Thank You Marilyn it's been a lot of fun  thank you for more information about  professor Miller and his research please  visit our website at macmillan report  yale edu be sure to join us  again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Denny  Macmillan Center for 